      "math_academy_id": "e-1608",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/77812c9ad896881463914cca5c2fa8b55b92f1be0fee2611390e9ffdca3ee40c.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/836c2d8c360f664eba78b0a64d0c9c0a6269dc797708fa95d680afe532bfe371.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{y+1}.$",
